# {{.AppName}}

Go **modular monolith** following **Clean Architecture**, built on [archkit]({{.Kit}}) and scaffolded with `archgen`.

## Run

```bash
cp .env.example .env
make db-up        # PostgreSQL in Docker
make run          # migrations are applied at startup (AUTO_MIGRATE=true)
curl localhost:8080/health/ready
```

## Structure

```text
cmd/api/main.go                 entry point
internal/app/                   composition root (bootstrap, dependencies, router, RBAC)
internal/modules/<module>/
    domain/                     aggregates, value objects, domain events, errors, repository ports
    application/commands/       write use cases
    application/queries/        read use cases
    application/ports/          interfaces the use cases need (implemented in infrastructure)
    infrastructure/persistence/ PostgreSQL (and in-memory) repositories
    interfaces/http/            HTTP adapters → commands/queries
    contract/                   the module's PUBLIC API (the only package other modules may import)
    module.go                   wires the module's layers
internal/shared/                technical code only (kernel, database, http, logging, observability)
migrations/                     SQL migrations (each module owns a PostgreSQL schema)
```

## Generate code

```bash
archgen module courses
archgen resource courses Course --fields "title:string:unique, summary:text:optional, level:enum(beginner|advanced), price:float, published:bool"
archgen command courses PublishCourse --fields "courseId:uuid"
archgen query courses CountCourses
archgen event users UserRegistered --fields "userId:uuid, email:email" --public
archgen subscribe enrollments users.UserRegistered
archgen contract users UserReader --resource User
archgen use enrollments users.UserReader      # inject that contract into enrollments
archgen port users PasswordHasher
archgen migration courses add_course_tags
archgen check           # enforce the architecture rules
```

## Rules (enforced by `archgen check`)

- The domain imports no infrastructure: no HTTP, SQL, drivers, or archkit infrastructure packages.
- The application layer never imports infrastructure or interfaces.
- The interfaces layer never imports infrastructure.
- A module imports another module **only** through `<module>/contract`.
- `internal/shared` never imports business modules.
